00:01:51.248 --> 00:01:54.163
"Homeroom with Sal," the podcast.

00:01:54.163 --> 00:01:57.713
So with that, I am very
excited to introduce

00:01:57.713 --> 00:01:59.722
both Carol and Vicky.

00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:02.311
Carol and Vicky, welcome.

00:02:02.311 --> 00:02:03.144
- Thank you.

00:02:03.144 --> 00:02:03.977
- Hi Sal.

00:02:03.977 --> 00:02:06.181
Great to be here with you.

00:02:06.181 --> 00:02:08.853
- So I wish I could have
two hours with each of you.

00:02:08.853 --> 00:02:11.035
There's a lot for us to cover.

00:02:11.035 --> 00:02:14.549
But maybe I'll start with you, Carol.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:17.435
I think a lot of folks
who've been in education.

00:02:17.435 --> 00:02:20.004
Know about "growth mindset."

00:02:20.004 --> 00:02:22.148
But if you could tell me,
for anyone who has been

00:02:22.148 --> 00:02:24.355
under a rock for the last several decades.

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:26.605
What is a "growth mindset."

00:02:33.251 --> 00:02:34.747
And why has it caught the "Zeitgeist"

00:02:34.747 --> 00:02:36.412
so to speak of education?

00:02:36.412 --> 00:02:37.847
And maybe a little bit of what are the

00:02:37.847 --> 00:02:39.112
misconceptions around it?

00:02:39.112 --> 00:02:40.616
(gasps)

00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:41.720
- Thanks Sal.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.302
A "growth mindset" is the belief that

00:02:45.302 --> 00:02:48.719
your fundamental abilities are not fixed.

00:02:49.693 --> 00:02:51.443
But can be developed.

00:02:53.632 --> 00:02:56.905
It's in contrast to a fixed mindset.

00:02:56.905 --> 00:03:01.738
Where, the belief is your
abilities are genetically based.

00:03:04.352 --> 00:03:06.102
Or in some way fixed.

00:03:06.972 --> 00:03:08.638
And, you are what you are.

00:03:08.638 --> 00:03:09.882
And you have what you have.

00:03:09.882 --> 00:03:12.452
And that's how you stay.

00:03:12.452 --> 00:03:16.822
I think the "growth mindset"
caught on for several reasons.

00:03:16.822 --> 00:03:17.943
First.

00:03:17.943 --> 00:03:21.192
We've done decades of work showing how,

00:03:21.192 --> 00:03:26.192
and students believe that their
abilities can be developed.

00:03:26.452 --> 00:03:29.102
They have greater motivation to learn.

00:03:29.102 --> 00:03:32.202
They're, less afraid
to take on challenges.

00:03:32.202 --> 00:03:35.412
Less discouraged by mistakes.

00:03:35.412 --> 00:03:37.312
They're developing something.

00:03:37.312 --> 00:03:41.479
They're not afraid of it's
being judged deficient.

00:03:44.045 --> 00:03:47.391
This was a new way of seeing students.

00:03:47.391 --> 00:03:51.058
Seeing them not as
people that you sort into

00:03:52.011 --> 00:03:54.242
the smart and not smart.

00:03:54.242 --> 00:03:56.492
But people you can develop.

00:03:58.898 --> 00:04:01.831
Help them realize their full potential.

00:04:01.831 --> 00:04:03.953
I think it's exciting for teachers

00:04:03.953 --> 00:04:08.203
to believe that every single
child in front of them

00:04:09.492 --> 00:04:12.992
has unknown potential that you can unlock.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:17.420
Now, some of the misconceptions.

00:04:19.032 --> 00:04:22.003
It's not always so intuitive.

00:04:22.003 --> 00:04:26.832
How you communicate a "growth
mindset" to your students.

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:31.832
So some teachers thought,
just tell them to work hard.

00:04:32.032 --> 00:04:33.762
Tell them you can learn anything.

00:04:33.762 --> 00:04:35.772
Tell them everyone's smart.

00:04:35.772 --> 00:04:39.272
But, what we have found over time is that.

00:04:41.892 --> 00:04:44.832
"Growth mindset" is a classroom culture.

00:04:44.832 --> 00:04:47.532
It's not something you say to kids.

00:04:47.532 --> 00:04:50.615
Or put a poster in front of the room.

00:04:51.761 --> 00:04:54.863
It's something that you
bake into the culture

00:04:54.863 --> 00:04:56.434
of the classroom.

00:04:56.434 --> 00:05:00.891
And have all kinds of
practices that support it.

00:05:00.891 --> 00:05:04.641
Like making sure every
student knows you care

00:05:05.621 --> 00:05:06.917
about their learning.

00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.941
Believe they can learn to a high level.

00:05:09.941 --> 00:05:14.531
Making sure they know
you think they can learn

00:05:14.531 --> 00:05:16.441
from their mistakes.

00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:20.090
The mistakes don't mean
they're incompetent.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.673
Letting kids redo work
to raise their grade

00:05:25.202 --> 00:05:27.601
and demonstrate their understanding.

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:32.121
Focusing on the progress,
not immediate performance.

00:05:32.121 --> 00:05:35.233
So now we're really focused on this idea

00:05:35.233 --> 00:05:39.181
of the "growth mindset,"
classroom culture.

00:05:39.181 --> 00:05:42.281
That fosters and empowers the students

00:05:42.281 --> 00:05:44.864
to use their "growth mindsets."

00:05:46.132 --> 00:05:47.550
- And one follow question before,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.472
I think what Vicky's work
is very complimentary

00:05:50.472 --> 00:05:51.973
to everything you just talked about.

00:05:51.973 --> 00:05:52.832
But I'm curious, Carol.

00:05:52.832 --> 00:05:53.792
And I've never asked you this.

00:05:53.792 --> 00:05:56.007
What got you into this research?

00:05:56.007 --> 00:05:58.532
What was the reason
that you kind of started

00:05:58.532 --> 00:05:59.622
formulating this whole theory

00:05:59.622 --> 00:06:02.872
around "growth mindset" and testing it?

00:06:04.518 --> 00:06:06.734
- If I can start in childhood.

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:09.401
But I'm not gonna go every year.

00:06:12.376 --> 00:06:15.954
And if you ask me who's my
most influential teacher.

00:06:15.954 --> 00:06:17.214
It's Mrs. Wilson.

00:06:17.214 --> 00:06:18.525
My 6th grade teacher.

00:06:18.525 --> 00:06:22.025
Who seated us around the room in IQ order.

00:06:24.034 --> 00:06:25.134
- [Sal] Wow.

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:28.860
- And I thought, "When you
try to ask a 6th grader,"

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.360
I thought, "This is so wrong."

00:06:32.849 --> 00:06:34.744
I know the kids in this class.

00:06:34.744 --> 00:06:36.659
I've been with them for years.

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:40.242
You can't sort them
point by point into IQ.

00:06:41.514 --> 00:06:45.215
Nonetheless, I was sucked into her system.

00:06:45.215 --> 00:06:49.445
I wanted to succeed within her framework.

00:06:49.445 --> 00:06:53.778
So years later, when I fell
in love with psychology.

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:58.206
And saw that you could use science

00:06:58.206 --> 00:07:01.456
to study people's beliefs, motivations.

00:07:03.336 --> 00:07:07.086
I thought, "I'm gonna
study why some children

00:07:08.809 --> 00:07:11.726
are able to fulfill their potential

00:07:12.657 --> 00:07:15.701
by taking on challenges
and sticking to them."

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:19.248
And other kids who may
be really, really skilled

00:07:19.248 --> 00:07:22.415
never achieve what they're capable of.

00:07:24.581 --> 00:07:26.627
And in graduate school.

00:07:26.627 --> 00:07:31.544
I embarked on the line of
research that I'm still pursuing.

00:07:33.142 --> 00:07:33.975
- It's fascinating.

00:07:33.975 --> 00:07:35.317
And I definitely wanna come back to that.

00:07:35.317 --> 00:07:37.613
But you know, Vicky.

00:07:37.613 --> 00:07:40.069
Tell us about "Escuela Nueva."

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:42.736
What was the reason you started it?

00:07:42.736 --> 00:07:45.986
And what is the core underlying principle

00:07:45.986 --> 00:07:48.403
of what's made it what it is?

00:07:49.572 --> 00:07:53.181
- "Escuela Nueva," word in Spanish.

00:07:53.181 --> 00:07:54.070
"New School."

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:54.982
Well, first of all.

00:07:54.982 --> 00:07:56.389
It's not so new anymore.

00:07:56.389 --> 00:07:59.690
It's one of the longest
bottom up innovations

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.126
to the developing world.

00:08:01.126 --> 00:08:02.659
That's still being sustained.

00:08:02.659 --> 00:08:04.878
It's like a pedagogical movement.

00:08:04.878 --> 00:08:08.547
Nothing new in the
philosophy of education.

00:08:08.547 --> 00:08:10.926
All the things we know
that the great pedagogue

00:08:10.926 --> 00:08:13.308
said at the beginning of the century.

00:08:13.308 --> 00:08:15.239
Good child centered learning.

00:08:15.239 --> 00:08:18.237
Not everybody learns the
same thing at the same time.

00:08:18.237 --> 00:08:19.772
Self-paced learning.

00:08:19.772 --> 00:08:22.002
Children learn looking into their eyes.

00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:25.126
Dialoguing and constructing
knowledge together.

00:08:25.126 --> 00:08:27.925
So it's, co-operative learning.

00:08:27.925 --> 00:08:31.782
A new role of the teacher, as
a great mentor, a facilitator.

00:08:31.782 --> 00:08:35.668
That has more time to really
focus on the human dimension.

00:08:35.668 --> 00:08:39.443
So we started thinking about
all these things by necessity.

00:08:39.443 --> 00:08:42.566
Because we started with the isolated

00:08:42.566 --> 00:08:44.733
rural-multi grade schools.

00:08:46.224 --> 00:08:49.143
Invisible schools, of
developing countries.

00:08:49.143 --> 00:08:51.572
And of course in Columbia,
where we started.

00:08:51.572 --> 00:08:55.264
And, basically we wanted
to introduce these concepts

00:08:55.264 --> 00:08:56.681
of good pedagogy.

00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:02.934
All these ideas exist, and
have existed for so many years.

00:09:02.934 --> 00:09:06.406
With John Dewey, with Maria
Montessori, Lev Vygotsky.

00:09:06.406 --> 00:09:07.239
All of these.

00:09:07.239 --> 00:09:09.596
But these ideas came
only to the elite schools

00:09:09.596 --> 00:09:10.726
in Latin America.

00:09:10.726 --> 00:09:12.326
Not to the poorest of the poor.

00:09:12.326 --> 00:09:14.509
So I'm originally a sociologist.

00:09:14.509 --> 00:09:18.166
And, but of course I
had a wonderful mother.

00:09:18.166 --> 00:09:19.957
She was a great teacher.

00:09:19.957 --> 00:09:21.394
That did a lot in Columbia.

00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:22.966
And founded teacher's colleges.

00:09:22.966 --> 00:09:26.178
And she was always talking
about child centered learning.

00:09:26.178 --> 00:09:28.358
And, so I had a contrast.

00:09:28.358 --> 00:09:31.486
Because of what I received
in good quality schools,

00:09:31.486 --> 00:09:32.319
private schools.

00:09:32.319 --> 00:09:34.010
And what I was seeing in reality.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.610
With the most isolated,
rural children of Columbia.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.377
So I wanted to introduce
the concept of equity.

00:09:41.377 --> 00:09:43.167
We do this in equity.

00:09:43.167 --> 00:09:45.877
I wanted to introduce
the concept of really,

00:09:45.877 --> 00:09:50.544
child centered learning to
improve quality in education.

00:09:52.667 --> 00:09:54.976
21st century skills.

00:09:54.976 --> 00:09:57.766
And of course the new role of the teacher.

00:09:57.766 --> 00:10:01.641
So these rural schools, there
were so many difficulties

00:10:01.641 --> 00:10:04.496
that we really had to
have a paradigm shift

00:10:04.496 --> 00:10:07.907
from transmission of knowledge to

00:10:07.907 --> 00:10:09.996
social construction of knowledge.

00:10:09.996 --> 00:10:12.446
And we really wanted to incorporate

00:10:12.446 --> 00:10:15.154
and strengthen the relationship between

00:10:15.154 --> 00:10:18.896
citizenship building and your pedagogy.

00:10:18.896 --> 00:10:22.696
So I guess these ideas
and the necessity for us.

00:10:22.696 --> 00:10:24.956
"Necessity was the mother of innovation."

00:10:24.956 --> 00:10:26.946
So I say it's a little bit,

00:10:26.946 --> 00:10:29.156
like we had to learn how to transform

00:10:29.156 --> 00:10:33.406
all this complexity into
simple, manageable action.

00:10:34.306 --> 00:10:37.255
So that anybody, any
teacher without having a PhD

00:10:37.255 --> 00:10:39.096
could have results.

00:10:39.096 --> 00:10:41.038
So I think these are the little tricks

00:10:41.038 --> 00:10:43.037
we have in "Escuela Nueva."

00:10:43.037 --> 00:10:44.596
Which means "New School."

00:10:44.596 --> 00:10:45.896
And as I said is one of the longest ones

00:10:45.896 --> 00:10:48.272
innovations of the developing world.

00:10:48.272 --> 00:10:49.232
That's still being sustained.

00:10:49.232 --> 00:10:52.817
And that has inspired of course
many innovations worldwide.

00:10:52.817 --> 00:10:56.316
Like the Vietnamese, for example.

00:10:56.316 --> 00:10:58.841
From Columbia to Vietnam.

00:10:58.841 --> 00:11:02.093
- And now there's tens of
thousands of these schools.

00:11:02.093 --> 00:11:04.061
I mean, it is at scale.

00:11:04.061 --> 00:11:07.593
For anyone who hasn't seen a "New School."

00:11:07.593 --> 00:11:09.012
An "Escuela Nueva."

00:11:09.012 --> 00:11:11.272
What would it look like?

00:11:11.272 --> 00:11:14.622
It sounds very different than
that teacher that Carol had.

00:11:14.622 --> 00:11:16.718
Lined up all those students by IQ.

00:11:16.718 --> 00:11:17.643
What does it look like?

00:11:17.643 --> 00:11:20.522
What would you hear and see?

00:11:20.522 --> 00:11:21.355
- Totally.

00:11:21.355 --> 00:11:24.554
When you go into a
"Escuela Nueva," school.

00:11:24.554 --> 00:11:28.233
You see, it's an example
of democracy in a way.

00:11:28.233 --> 00:11:29.459
By the way, we're going through all these

00:11:29.459 --> 00:11:31.080
crisis and processes.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.680
But you see some children working

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.002
in some things by themselves, in pairs.

00:11:36.002 --> 00:11:38.443
Others working in small groups.

00:11:38.443 --> 00:11:41.082
Some are going to their learning corners.

00:11:41.082 --> 00:11:43.813
It's a learning environment
that has changed.

00:11:43.813 --> 00:11:46.182
They're permanently
talking and dialoguing.

00:11:46.182 --> 00:11:48.066
And you see a teacher walking around

00:11:48.066 --> 00:11:50.032
when children call on the teacher.

00:11:50.032 --> 00:11:51.593
Not the other way around.

00:11:51.593 --> 00:11:53.082
When the children call on the teacher.

00:11:53.082 --> 00:11:55.148
Cause we really want it
to be child centered.

00:11:55.148 --> 00:11:58.942
You see the teacher going
through the different groups.

00:11:58.942 --> 00:12:00.749
Talking individually with some.

00:12:00.749 --> 00:12:02.216
Talking in pairs with others.

00:12:02.216 --> 00:12:04.479
You see some children
going out of the classroom

00:12:04.479 --> 00:12:06.336
to do an experiment by themselves.

00:12:06.336 --> 00:12:09.076
They're working with
their learning guides.

00:12:09.076 --> 00:12:12.566
They have their, a lot of
self-governing instruments

00:12:12.566 --> 00:12:16.956
to promote a school
government and democracy.

00:12:16.956 --> 00:12:19.416
And we really want to
incorporate a culture

00:12:19.416 --> 00:12:20.829
of participation.

00:12:20.829 --> 00:12:24.737
So they're many little
tricks that we see that

00:12:24.737 --> 00:12:27.059
strengthened self-esteem.

00:12:27.059 --> 00:12:29.026
Discovery learning.

00:12:29.026 --> 00:12:30.211
Child-centered.

00:12:30.211 --> 00:12:32.012
Their self-concept.

00:12:32.012 --> 00:12:35.099
We want them to really enjoy learning.

00:12:35.099 --> 00:12:38.546
And I think we have seen
this in these very isolated

00:12:38.546 --> 00:12:41.579
rural schools of Columbia
and of other countries.

00:12:41.579 --> 00:12:43.970
Where we have worked with.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.881
- And how did you pull this off?

00:12:46.881 --> 00:12:48.485
I mean, I think that's
the most important thing

00:12:48.485 --> 00:12:49.535
on a lot of levels.

00:12:49.535 --> 00:12:51.202
How did this happen?

00:12:52.185 --> 00:12:53.018
I mean I could imagine,

00:12:53.018 --> 00:12:54.670
especially if you're going to rural areas.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:58.150
The teacher's notion of
what a teacher does is very,

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.741
probably very traditional.

00:12:59.741 --> 00:13:01.697
Someone at a chalkboard, lecturing.

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:04.610
While students have fingers
on lips and listening.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:08.858
How were you able to manage
the controlled chaos?

00:13:08.858 --> 00:13:09.691
So to speak.

00:13:09.691 --> 00:13:12.527
Of multiple students working
at their own time and pace.

00:13:12.527 --> 00:13:17.194
And you did this even before
Ed Tech became a real tool.

00:13:18.122 --> 00:13:19.328
Explain the logistics of it.

00:13:19.328 --> 00:13:20.512
And then I actually wanna connect it.

00:13:20.512 --> 00:13:22.002
Cause it sounds very complimentary

00:13:22.002 --> 00:13:24.157
to everything that Carol was mentioning.

00:13:24.157 --> 00:13:25.110
- Totally, totally.

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.410
That's why we say with Carol,

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.446
we fell in love with each other.

00:13:27.446 --> 00:13:31.400
Because Carol coming from such
solid scientific research.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.483
And me coming from such
necessity and complexity.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:42.995
The question you asked me is,

00:13:42.995 --> 00:13:45.624
"How do they know what they're
doing and how they are?"

00:13:45.624 --> 00:13:47.269
Well, we had to develop.

00:13:47.269 --> 00:13:48.102
First of all.

00:13:48.102 --> 00:13:51.038
We had to change the teacher's attitudes.

00:13:51.038 --> 00:13:54.955
We wanted to make sure
that teachers be trained

00:13:55.848 --> 00:13:58.860
with the same methodology
they will be using

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.410
with their students.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:03.419
Because we're asking
teachers to make the change.

00:14:03.419 --> 00:14:05.739
And they've been exposed
to very traditional

00:14:05.739 --> 00:14:09.188
teacher centered learning
when they were students.

00:14:09.188 --> 00:14:10.800
When they were studying.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.406
So the first thing we had to do was.

00:14:12.406 --> 00:14:16.359
Try to change what most
faculties of education,

00:14:16.359 --> 00:14:18.539
and teachers colleges are not doing.

00:14:18.539 --> 00:14:22.959
So we wanted, that teachers
go through the same process

00:14:22.959 --> 00:14:25.059
they will be doing with their children.

00:14:25.059 --> 00:14:25.892
Second.

00:14:25.892 --> 00:14:27.609
We would like them to see a demo school.

00:14:27.609 --> 00:14:29.976
So that they would say, "We can do it."

00:14:29.976 --> 00:14:31.371
"It's nothing complicated."

00:14:31.371 --> 00:14:32.911
"We can perfectly do this."

00:14:32.911 --> 00:14:34.415
And the third thing was.

00:14:34.415 --> 00:14:36.065
Not leave the teachers alone.

00:14:36.065 --> 00:14:38.247
We had to create a community of practice.

00:14:38.247 --> 00:14:40.224
So because teachers learn
more from each other

00:14:40.224 --> 00:14:41.415
than from experts.

00:14:41.415 --> 00:14:43.508
And of course the whole idea was introduce

00:14:43.508 --> 00:14:45.597
the concept of flexibility.

00:14:45.597 --> 00:14:47.264
Act, time and space.

00:14:49.307 --> 00:14:51.440
We had to introduce a whole
concept of flexibility

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.000
in these terms.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:56.406
And because, learning is not a place.

00:14:56.406 --> 00:14:58.094
It's an activity.

00:14:58.094 --> 00:14:58.927
Okay?

00:14:58.927 --> 00:15:01.063
So, we have learned that many of the most

00:15:01.063 --> 00:15:03.164
important activities
take place in their homes

00:15:03.164 --> 00:15:04.187
with their parents.

00:15:04.187 --> 00:15:07.096
And so we just wanted
to make sure that all

00:15:07.096 --> 00:15:10.151
of these ideas were incorporated
in what we were doing.

00:15:10.151 --> 00:15:13.517
And we were really transforming
all this complexity

00:15:13.517 --> 00:15:16.182
into simple, manageable action.

00:15:16.182 --> 00:15:17.587
We have to make it simple.

00:15:17.587 --> 00:15:21.670
Simple, replicable,
scalable, and cost-effective.

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:26.728
- No, super powerful.

00:15:26.728 --> 00:15:29.800
I'm already getting a ton of
questions from social media.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:31.988
So I wanna make sure I get to
as many of these as possible.

00:15:31.988 --> 00:15:33.188
And these are connected to the work

00:15:33.188 --> 00:15:36.054
that both of y'all are doing.

00:15:36.054 --> 00:15:36.887
From YouTube.

00:15:36.887 --> 00:15:39.212
Joshua Kadavul asks,

00:15:39.212 --> 00:15:41.131
"It's very difficult to motivate ourselves

00:15:41.131 --> 00:15:42.675
into a growth mindset."

00:15:42.675 --> 00:15:45.544
"What do you suggest to help
us get into that mindset?"

00:15:45.544 --> 00:15:46.501
I'll start with Carol.

00:15:46.501 --> 00:15:49.491
Who knows the science
and the research here.

00:15:49.491 --> 00:15:50.324
But then after that.

00:15:50.324 --> 00:15:51.764
I'd also love to learn from Vicky.

00:15:51.764 --> 00:15:54.244
Because, if all of your
students have this agency.

00:15:54.244 --> 00:15:55.078
And it's student centered learning.

00:15:55.078 --> 00:15:56.782
I'm assuming a lot of them need

00:15:56.782 --> 00:15:58.156
the same "growth mindset" as well.

00:15:58.156 --> 00:16:00.207
But Carol, what advice
do you have for Josh

00:16:00.207 --> 00:16:04.029
or anyone listening on, how
do you get into that mindset?

00:16:04.029 --> 00:16:06.228
- How old is Josh?

00:16:06.228 --> 00:16:07.828
- [Sal] I have no idea.

00:16:07.828 --> 00:16:09.161
He's on YouTube.

00:16:10.209 --> 00:16:11.324
- Okay Josh.

00:16:11.324 --> 00:16:14.333
Whether you're a student or an adult.

00:16:14.333 --> 00:16:18.583
You have to ask yourself,
"What do you want to do?"

00:16:21.541 --> 00:16:23.617
"What do you care about?"

00:16:23.617 --> 00:16:28.617
"What do you want to contribute
to the world in your life?"

00:16:29.291 --> 00:16:31.874
And then, "How do I get there?"

00:16:33.613 --> 00:16:35.536
You'll need to take on challenges.

00:16:35.536 --> 00:16:38.369
You'll need to know that everybody

00:16:39.551 --> 00:16:41.659
struggles through challenges.

00:16:41.659 --> 00:16:43.784
Everybody has setbacks.

00:16:43.784 --> 00:16:45.472
It's part of learning.

00:16:45.472 --> 00:16:49.889
Your fixed mindset voice
will come back to you a lot.

00:16:51.107 --> 00:16:53.121
And say, "This is too hard."

00:16:53.121 --> 00:16:54.394
"You were unrealistic."

00:16:54.394 --> 00:16:55.854
"You can't do that."

00:16:55.854 --> 00:16:58.993
And you have to talk back to it, not.

00:16:58.993 --> 00:16:59.826
"Go away."

00:16:59.826 --> 00:17:00.659
"I don't like you."

00:17:00.659 --> 00:17:02.233
"You're in my way."

00:17:02.233 --> 00:17:04.675
But, "Hey, I wanna try this."

00:17:04.675 --> 00:17:09.329
"Can you come on board
and collaborate with me?"

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:11.746
"Can you contribute with me?"

00:17:13.593 --> 00:17:15.125
"Can you let me try this?"

00:17:15.125 --> 00:17:18.125
So, where we all have both mindsets.

00:17:20.173 --> 00:17:22.543
The fixed mindset is
ready to track back in

00:17:22.543 --> 00:17:26.592
and discourage you, when things are hard.

00:17:26.592 --> 00:17:28.492
Or you have a failure.

00:17:28.492 --> 00:17:31.242
But, you have to get it on board.

00:17:32.221 --> 00:17:34.543
Get it on your side.

00:17:34.543 --> 00:17:38.062
And keep in mind, what
you're trying to accomplish.

00:17:38.062 --> 00:17:40.395
And who you're doing it for.

00:17:43.042 --> 00:17:45.042
- And Vicky, I'll actually
add this extra question

00:17:45.042 --> 00:17:48.263
from YouTube, and it actually
turns out Josh is 16.

00:17:48.263 --> 00:17:49.713
So I guessed correctly somehow.

00:17:49.713 --> 00:17:52.133
I'm clairvoyant in some way.

00:17:52.133 --> 00:17:54.003
But from YouTube, there's
a related question.

00:17:54.003 --> 00:17:54.883
Julian Miller asks,

00:17:54.883 --> 00:17:58.633
"Mrs. Dweck and Vicky,
how would you go about

00:17:59.963 --> 00:18:01.878
getting a younger student
with low self-esteem

00:18:01.878 --> 00:18:03.343
to gain a growth mindset?"

00:18:03.343 --> 00:18:04.818
And Vicky, I love your take first.

00:18:04.818 --> 00:18:07.556
Because, I'm assuming you've
essentially had to do this.

00:18:07.556 --> 00:18:10.069
I don't know when you
started "Escuela Nueva."

00:18:10.069 --> 00:18:12.709
Whether you thought that you
were doing "growth mindset."

00:18:12.709 --> 00:18:14.589
But now there's the science
from Carol behind it.

00:18:14.589 --> 00:18:16.984
But how have you thought
about motivating kids,

00:18:16.984 --> 00:18:20.852
reinforcing a "growth mindset"
versus a "fixed mindset?"

00:18:20.852 --> 00:18:21.685
- Yes.

00:18:21.685 --> 00:18:24.095
I think the first thing
we wanted to make sure is

00:18:24.095 --> 00:18:26.157
that children love school.

00:18:26.157 --> 00:18:29.443
That they would have a nice environment

00:18:29.443 --> 00:18:32.943
despite that it's a rural isolated school.

00:18:34.505 --> 00:18:37.213
That would be a nice pleasant environment.

00:18:37.213 --> 00:18:39.973
So, I think that's crucial.

00:18:39.973 --> 00:18:40.806
The second thing was.

00:18:40.806 --> 00:18:44.283
That they would feel the
love from their teacher.

00:18:44.283 --> 00:18:45.423
Not classifying them.

00:18:45.423 --> 00:18:48.483
But a teacher that would
always, open the doors to them.

00:18:48.483 --> 00:18:51.613
And give them the love and
affection they're needing.

00:18:51.613 --> 00:18:54.993
Cause they're really needing
so much affection and love.

00:18:54.993 --> 00:18:57.938
And a nice warm place and an environment

00:18:57.938 --> 00:18:59.855
where they can be safe.

00:19:01.919 --> 00:19:04.940
A wonderful aspect was,
to motivate children.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:06.803
To intrinsic motivation.

00:19:06.803 --> 00:19:07.636
I'm not a psychologist.

00:19:07.636 --> 00:19:09.055
I don't know much about it.

00:19:09.055 --> 00:19:12.826
But we wanted children to have
a little bit of self-concept.

00:19:12.826 --> 00:19:14.918
To know that they could be happy learning.

00:19:14.918 --> 00:19:18.574
And we developed all these
very simple instruments

00:19:18.574 --> 00:19:21.036
of, to promote autonomy.

00:19:21.036 --> 00:19:23.497
To promote self-determination.

00:19:23.497 --> 00:19:27.080
The flexibility that
all children can learn

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:29.675
if you give them the time.

00:19:29.675 --> 00:19:33.100
And that they don't have to
compete with anybody else.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:35.613
But that they can go on their own rhythm.

00:19:35.613 --> 00:19:39.475
And the motivation, the self-regulation.

00:19:39.475 --> 00:19:40.697
They're many, many instruments

00:19:40.697 --> 00:19:42.433
of self governing instruments.

00:19:42.433 --> 00:19:44.643
Where children control
their own attendance.

00:19:44.643 --> 00:19:47.523
Where they have all
these simple instruments

00:19:47.523 --> 00:19:49.246
of the school governments.

00:19:49.246 --> 00:19:51.797
Where children elected, and are elected.

00:19:51.797 --> 00:19:54.411
And they have the school
government, with their projects.

00:19:54.411 --> 00:19:56.900
Concrete projects they
have with their parents

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:58.390
and their community.

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:00.511
So we had to think of
all these little tricks

00:20:00.511 --> 00:20:03.820
to make sure that any teacher could have

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:06.530
a wonderful classroom
and a nice environment.

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:08.100
That all children could learn

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:12.885
if you give them the time
and a nice safe environment.

00:20:12.885 --> 00:20:16.178
So it's a really a whole concept
of child-friendly concept.

00:20:16.178 --> 00:20:18.789
That we wanted to embrace.

00:20:18.789 --> 00:20:20.221
So the school was a place where children

00:20:20.221 --> 00:20:22.277
didn't want to leave.

00:20:22.277 --> 00:20:23.744
They wanted to stay.

00:20:23.744 --> 00:20:25.853
Because they had a loving teacher.

00:20:25.853 --> 00:20:29.568
And they had their classmates
where they worked together.

00:20:29.568 --> 00:20:31.785
And they learn to dialogue.

00:20:31.785 --> 00:20:33.553
So I mean, they're very simple tricks

00:20:33.553 --> 00:20:35.569
that we had to incorporate.

00:20:35.569 --> 00:20:38.224
But they've demonstrated
that it can be done.

00:20:38.224 --> 00:20:42.460
Yes, we can introduce
quality, reduce inequity.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:46.102
Incorporate leadership
skills from the beginning.

00:20:46.102 --> 00:20:47.696
It's amazing to see these little children

00:20:47.696 --> 00:20:52.529
taking responsibilities and
assuming the responsibilities.

00:20:54.284 --> 00:20:57.089
And leading others and
learning to work in teams.

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.922
I have to say I had a
professor at Stanford.

00:20:59.922 --> 00:21:01.105
Elizabeth Cohen.

00:21:01.105 --> 00:21:05.415
For the first time I learned
about corporative learning.

00:21:05.415 --> 00:21:09.514
Here in Latin America we talk
about personalized learning.

00:21:09.514 --> 00:21:11.485
But not corporative learning.

00:21:11.485 --> 00:21:13.092
And we have seen the results.

00:21:13.092 --> 00:21:15.104
Because, let's say we've always have tried

00:21:15.104 --> 00:21:18.018
to have some empirical research behind.

00:21:18.018 --> 00:21:20.114
So for example, the University of London

00:21:20.114 --> 00:21:22.911
published one of our first research papers

00:21:22.911 --> 00:21:25.330
demonstrating the impact
of "Escuela Nueva."

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:28.199
On peaceful, social
interaction of children.

00:21:28.199 --> 00:21:29.701
Not only on academic achievement.

00:21:29.701 --> 00:21:32.117
But on peaceful behavior.

00:21:32.117 --> 00:21:35.197
And I think this is so important,
coming out of Columbia.

00:21:35.197 --> 00:21:36.857
Very important.

00:21:36.857 --> 00:21:38.127
- No, thanks for that Vicky.

00:21:38.127 --> 00:21:39.638
And Carol, same question.

00:21:39.638 --> 00:21:42.301
Say there's a younger student that,

00:21:42.301 --> 00:21:44.587
how does a parent or a teacher

00:21:44.587 --> 00:21:46.267
or anyone who cares about them.

00:21:46.267 --> 00:21:49.237
The question was specifically if

00:21:49.237 --> 00:21:51.369
they have a low self-esteem
to have a "growth mindset."

00:21:51.369 --> 00:21:54.157
Sometimes you've been
that kid where we're like,

00:21:54.157 --> 00:21:56.212
"Oh, I'm not good at this."

00:21:56.212 --> 00:21:58.261
"My brain isn't wired for that."

00:21:58.261 --> 00:22:00.008
What do you tell them?

00:22:00.008 --> 00:22:01.354
What do you do?

00:22:01.354 --> 00:22:03.588
- Well first, I wanna
say one of the hundreds

00:22:03.588 --> 00:22:05.972
or even thousands things I love

00:22:05.972 --> 00:22:09.637
about Vicky's work and her schools.

00:22:09.637 --> 00:22:14.532
Is that she takes the fixed
mindset off the table.

00:22:14.532 --> 00:22:17.216
It doesn't occur to
students in that environment

00:22:17.216 --> 00:22:19.716
to worry about being measured.

00:22:21.373 --> 00:22:23.022
But, in our culture.

00:22:23.022 --> 00:22:25.312
It's really different.

00:22:25.312 --> 00:22:30.153
Parents worry about how
smart their children are.

00:22:30.153 --> 00:22:32.132
Parents measure them.

00:22:32.132 --> 00:22:35.382
Then they go to school, they're tested.

00:22:36.443 --> 00:22:38.648
They're compared to each other.

00:22:38.648 --> 00:22:41.862
Teachers often decide who's the smart

00:22:41.862 --> 00:22:44.012
and who's not as smart.

00:22:44.012 --> 00:22:46.345
So, it's not a surprise that

00:22:47.944 --> 00:22:52.432
we have a lot of young
students with low self-esteem.

00:22:52.432 --> 00:22:56.263
Now, my research and
the research of others

00:22:56.263 --> 00:22:59.444
like Eddie Brummelman in the Netherlands.

00:22:59.444 --> 00:23:02.586
Shows that sometimes people react to kids

00:23:02.586 --> 00:23:04.802
with low self-esteem.

00:23:04.802 --> 00:23:07.446
By telling them how smart they are.

00:23:07.446 --> 00:23:10.301
Or by over praising them.

00:23:10.301 --> 00:23:13.159
Which actually makes them more vulnerable

00:23:13.159 --> 00:23:15.242
to failure in the future.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:19.253
I would say the research suggests

00:23:19.253 --> 00:23:22.015
keep the focus on learning.

00:23:22.015 --> 00:23:24.197
Keep the focus on progress.

00:23:24.197 --> 00:23:27.922
Keep the focus on challenging yourself.

00:23:27.922 --> 00:23:31.255
Say to a child who did something easily.

00:23:32.099 --> 00:23:33.432
"Oh, I'm sorry."

00:23:34.763 --> 00:23:36.936
"I'm sorry, I wasted your time."

00:23:36.936 --> 00:23:39.112
"That was too easy for you."

00:23:39.112 --> 00:23:40.345
"You must have been bored."

00:23:40.345 --> 00:23:42.609
Let's do something fun.

00:23:42.609 --> 00:23:45.038
Something you can learn from.

00:23:45.038 --> 00:23:49.539
If the child gets stuck and
discouraged on something.

00:23:49.539 --> 00:23:51.456
Be that backup support.

00:23:52.501 --> 00:23:54.513
Like Vicky's teachers.

00:23:54.513 --> 00:23:56.686
Let's see what you've done.

00:23:56.686 --> 00:23:59.737
What can you think of, of doing next?

00:23:59.737 --> 00:24:00.987
Let's try that.

00:24:02.001 --> 00:24:07.001
So this detective process of
what you didn't understand

00:24:07.131 --> 00:24:09.648
and how you can understand it.

00:24:09.648 --> 00:24:11.981
Make that whole process fun.

00:24:13.071 --> 00:24:16.895
We've seen that parents
who believe that failure

00:24:16.895 --> 00:24:19.812
can inspire their child's learning.

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:24.652
Are much better mentors to their kids.

00:24:24.652 --> 00:24:28.574
Than parents who worry
that their child's failures

00:24:28.574 --> 00:24:29.824
will harm them.

00:24:30.678 --> 00:24:33.261
And make them feel incompetent.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.723
So this whole focus on
challenge, progress.

00:24:39.577 --> 00:24:41.494
Learning from setbacks.

00:24:44.271 --> 00:24:48.354
Is, what goes into this
natural love of learning.

00:24:49.277 --> 00:24:52.940
As opposed to the self doubt
that comes from feeling

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:54.773
measured and compared.

00:24:58.463 --> 00:24:59.296
- Yeah.

00:24:59.296 --> 00:25:00.281
And there's a lot of questions coming in.

00:25:00.281 --> 00:25:03.186
I mean, this resonates
with anyone listening.

00:25:03.186 --> 00:25:05.970
Anyone who at any level of depth on this.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:08.512
And I think a lot of the
questions are the flavor of,

00:25:08.512 --> 00:25:11.792
how do we actually put this
out there in the world?

00:25:11.792 --> 00:25:14.168
We've talked about how you
can do it as an individual.

00:25:14.168 --> 00:25:15.863
Or how you could even
change your own mindset.

00:25:15.863 --> 00:25:17.144
We know we have this question.

00:25:17.144 --> 00:25:19.104
I'm paraphrasing from
YouTube, Jamal Shahid.

00:25:19.104 --> 00:25:22.094
"How would this look in an urban school?"

00:25:22.094 --> 00:25:24.909
From YouTube, Manish Raj Jaiswal.

00:25:24.909 --> 00:25:28.809
They're talking about
stereotypical South Asian parents.

00:25:28.809 --> 00:25:32.181
That are more grades oriented
versus skill oriented.

00:25:32.181 --> 00:25:33.249
How do you change that?

00:25:33.249 --> 00:25:36.816
So the broader question
is, how do you scale this?

00:25:36.816 --> 00:25:40.350
Cause both of y'all have scaled
this idea in different ways.

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.461
Carol, where do you think we are?

00:25:43.461 --> 00:25:45.065
Versus where do you think we can be?

00:25:45.065 --> 00:25:46.526
And I'll ask Vicky the same question.

00:25:46.526 --> 00:25:49.168
Where we are versus where we could be?

00:25:49.168 --> 00:25:51.736
- I'll tell you exactly where we are.

00:25:51.736 --> 00:25:56.446
We have shown that direct
to student "growth mindset"

00:25:56.446 --> 00:25:57.946
programs can work.

00:25:59.676 --> 00:26:02.626
They can raise the achievement.

00:26:02.626 --> 00:26:05.170
The grades of lower achievers.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:08.409
They can inspire even higher achievers

00:26:08.409 --> 00:26:11.326
to take more advanced math courses.

00:26:12.936 --> 00:26:16.269
But, if a student has taken this program

00:26:18.496 --> 00:26:22.870
and goes back into say a
mass classroom with a teacher

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:26.094
who's created a fixed minded culture.

00:26:26.094 --> 00:26:28.509
They can't put that into practice.

00:26:28.509 --> 00:26:30.574
They can't thrive there.

00:26:30.574 --> 00:26:33.034
In the special way that
their "growth mindset"

00:26:33.034 --> 00:26:35.862
might've allowed them to.

00:26:35.862 --> 00:26:38.202
So then we realized.

00:26:38.202 --> 00:26:39.604
(gasps)

00:26:39.604 --> 00:26:43.021
We have to create a program for teachers.

00:26:45.348 --> 00:26:49.515
Just the way Vicky has
created a scalable program.

00:26:53.453 --> 00:26:57.932
We realized David Yeager,
Mary Murphy and I.

00:26:57.932 --> 00:27:01.015
We realized we've got to have modules

00:27:04.134 --> 00:27:05.786
that can scale up.

00:27:05.786 --> 00:27:09.031
That we can disseminate widely.

00:27:09.031 --> 00:27:12.198
That will prevent teachers from trying

00:27:14.384 --> 00:27:18.033
to implement it in ways that don't work.

00:27:18.033 --> 00:27:20.996
We're in the middle of that now.

00:27:20.996 --> 00:27:25.413
Vicky is a valued, valued
consultant in this project.

00:27:26.319 --> 00:27:28.652
This fall, we're gonna test.

00:27:32.675 --> 00:27:37.099
Administer and test the
first version of this.

00:27:37.099 --> 00:27:40.269
We'll do this the next year as well.

00:27:40.269 --> 00:27:41.994
When it's tested and ready.

00:27:41.994 --> 00:27:46.215
It will be available at
no cost to all teachers.

00:27:46.215 --> 00:27:49.798
We're doing it on an
adolescent population.

00:27:51.229 --> 00:27:54.160
It's the hardest thing we've ever done.

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:57.872
It's the most important
thing we've ever done.

00:27:57.872 --> 00:28:01.289
And we're working on it around the clock.

00:28:02.829 --> 00:28:04.159
- No, thanks for that Carol.

00:28:04.159 --> 00:28:04.992
And Vicky.

00:28:04.992 --> 00:28:06.137
I mean, you're already at the scale.

00:28:06.137 --> 00:28:07.645
I mean, tell me what scale you're at.

00:28:07.645 --> 00:28:09.434
I've read tens of thousands of schools.

00:28:09.434 --> 00:28:10.630
I don't know how many students that is.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.060
But I'm guessing hundreds of thousands

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:14.342
or millions of students across countries.

00:28:14.342 --> 00:28:16.091
So you're already a lot of scale.

00:28:16.091 --> 00:28:18.941
But how far do you think
you're on this journey?

00:28:18.941 --> 00:28:20.624
Where do you think we
could be in 5 or 10 years?

00:28:20.624 --> 00:28:22.041
What's your hope?

00:28:22.878 --> 00:28:25.393
- My hope is that we can
continue to move forward.

00:28:25.393 --> 00:28:28.792
However, they're many lessons
learned from this process.

00:28:28.792 --> 00:28:31.906
Of how to work with whom do you work with.

00:28:31.906 --> 00:28:35.586
Going back to your
initial question to Carol.

00:28:35.586 --> 00:28:39.669
We sort of had to also
modularize the curriculum.

00:28:40.986 --> 00:28:43.986
Curriculums they're like "big beasts."

00:28:43.986 --> 00:28:46.596
How do you tackle a national curriculum?

00:28:46.596 --> 00:28:49.256
So the first thing is we had
to break it up in chunks.

00:28:49.256 --> 00:28:52.673
Modularize it, to facilitate flexibility.

00:28:53.636 --> 00:28:55.462
Because we wanted to
incorporate the concept

00:28:55.462 --> 00:28:58.902
of self-paced learning and flexibility.

00:28:58.902 --> 00:29:01.704
So it's an important aspect
of the "Escuela Nueva."

00:29:01.704 --> 00:29:03.391
So the student's ability to progress

00:29:03.391 --> 00:29:05.318
through their learning materials.

00:29:05.318 --> 00:29:06.554
Their learning guides.

00:29:06.554 --> 00:29:09.752
Using both self assessments
and group assessments.

00:29:09.752 --> 00:29:14.089
But we believe that this
flexibility has helped a lot.

00:29:14.089 --> 00:29:15.040
Because in this way.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:17.256
Students know that they can achieve.

00:29:17.256 --> 00:29:20.013
Even if it was going a little bit slower.

00:29:20.013 --> 00:29:22.309
So it's the whole concept of flexibility

00:29:22.309 --> 00:29:25.969
that we had to introduce
in the curriculum.

00:29:25.969 --> 00:29:28.190
And the way we work with children.

00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:29.136
In everything.

00:29:29.136 --> 00:29:31.390
So this was for us really, really crucial.

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:35.171
And of course, I mean when we scaled up.

00:29:35.171 --> 00:29:37.299
We learned to work with governments.

00:29:37.299 --> 00:29:39.454
Which is something that
I have also learned.

00:29:39.454 --> 00:29:41.013
You have to work with governments.

00:29:41.013 --> 00:29:44.136
Because we learn how to
make it a national policy.

00:29:44.136 --> 00:29:48.553
But you need governments
for big impact and coverage.

00:29:50.136 --> 00:29:53.246
But you need the role of civil society

00:29:53.246 --> 00:29:56.176
and public private
partnerships for two key words.

00:29:56.176 --> 00:29:58.336
Quality and sustainability.

00:29:58.336 --> 00:30:00.335
They're many countries
where we have started

00:30:00.335 --> 00:30:01.875
"Escuela Nueva."

00:30:01.875 --> 00:30:02.708
In Brazil.

00:30:02.708 --> 00:30:03.541
In Guatemala.

00:30:03.541 --> 00:30:05.104
And all these places in Latin America.

00:30:05.104 --> 00:30:07.565
Beyond, we've inspired in Zambia.

00:30:07.565 --> 00:30:09.625
But the thing is that.

00:30:09.625 --> 00:30:12.475
One of the big issues is that

00:30:12.475 --> 00:30:14.970
we have inspired many countries.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:17.484
But, you get that the project started.

00:30:17.484 --> 00:30:18.375
It starts moving on.

00:30:18.375 --> 00:30:20.175
And then governments change.

00:30:20.175 --> 00:30:23.004
So this is another lesson
learned from all this process.

00:30:23.004 --> 00:30:25.184
Not only the, let's say
the curriculum dimensions

00:30:25.184 --> 00:30:27.394
of how to modularize curriculums.

00:30:27.394 --> 00:30:28.984
And make them flexible to adapt.

00:30:28.984 --> 00:30:30.615
That the school adapts to the children

00:30:30.615 --> 00:30:32.525
and not the other way around.

00:30:32.525 --> 00:30:35.408
Because by the way, I have
to highlight the initial way

00:30:35.408 --> 00:30:37.500
when we had to make it flexible.

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:40.554
Because children in rural areas
have to walk long distances.

00:30:40.554 --> 00:30:43.271
Because children
participate in picking crops

00:30:43.271 --> 00:30:44.750
with their parents.

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:47.083
So it's a mobile population.

00:30:48.727 --> 00:30:51.345
By the way, we have
adopted "Escuela Nueva"

00:30:51.345 --> 00:30:52.572
to urban contexts.

00:30:52.572 --> 00:30:53.800
"Escuela Activa Urbana."

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:56.981
And especially for migrants,
and displaced populations.

00:30:56.981 --> 00:30:58.771
Right now we're working with many, many

00:30:58.771 --> 00:31:01.224
Venezuelan migrants in Colombia.

00:31:01.224 --> 00:31:03.229
And it's been a successful program.

00:31:03.229 --> 00:31:06.267
Where we adapt, this is
in the cities of Columbia.

00:31:06.267 --> 00:31:07.368
Not in the rural areas.

00:31:07.368 --> 00:31:10.289
So this is where we adapt
to the needs of the child.

00:31:10.289 --> 00:31:11.670
And not the other way around.

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:14.220
That the school really
is open and flexible.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:17.070
And this is what we
really have tried to do.

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:18.689
And to maintain this flexibility.

00:31:18.689 --> 00:31:20.899
I don't know if I answered
your question correctly.

00:31:20.899 --> 00:31:23.566
But I picked up on other things.

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:26.368
- No, that was incredibly valuable.

00:31:26.368 --> 00:31:27.891
And this is conversation,

00:31:27.891 --> 00:31:29.585
I thought we were like
five minutes into it.

00:31:29.585 --> 00:31:30.486
Cause I'm so intrigued.

00:31:30.486 --> 00:31:34.386
And I realized that we're
pretty much out of time.

00:31:34.386 --> 00:31:35.476
And I apologize to everyone.

00:31:35.476 --> 00:31:36.423
There's so many good questions

00:31:36.423 --> 00:31:38.164
that have been coming in on social media.

00:31:38.164 --> 00:31:40.733
But, I'll leave it up to y'all.

00:31:40.733 --> 00:31:44.922
Maybe a last word from
either of you on messages

00:31:44.922 --> 00:31:48.262
you have for folks who are both
fascinated by these models.

00:31:48.262 --> 00:31:49.902
Fascinated by "growth mindset."

00:31:49.902 --> 00:31:52.818
But also fascinated by
y'all as individuals.

00:31:52.818 --> 00:31:54.269
And are looking to have
similar impact in their life.

00:31:54.269 --> 00:31:57.125
What messages would you have for them.

00:31:57.125 --> 00:31:58.866
Maybe Carol start.

00:31:58.866 --> 00:32:00.466
And then followed by Vicky.

00:32:00.466 --> 00:32:01.299
(gasps)

00:32:01.299 --> 00:32:02.882
- That's a big one.

00:32:06.914 --> 00:32:07.747
First.

00:32:07.747 --> 00:32:10.744
I just want to underscore
something Vicky said.

00:32:10.744 --> 00:32:13.827
How important equity is in education.

00:32:14.894 --> 00:32:19.611
How important it is for schools
to adopt to the culture.

00:32:19.611 --> 00:32:23.194
Adapt to the culture
and needs of students.

00:32:24.704 --> 00:32:28.726
And some "growth mindset," a
"growth mindset" assessment

00:32:28.726 --> 00:32:32.095
into a recent survey and testing.

00:32:32.095 --> 00:32:35.355
And found that "growth mindset" not only

00:32:35.355 --> 00:32:37.188
predicted achievement.

00:32:39.035 --> 00:32:41.725
And much or most of the world of the

00:32:41.725 --> 00:32:43.975
79 countries and economies.

00:32:46.207 --> 00:32:49.540
But also motivation to learn, wellbeing.

00:32:50.674 --> 00:32:54.985
It gave a bigger boost
to vulnerable students,

00:32:54.985 --> 00:32:56.902
low income, immigrants.

00:32:57.767 --> 00:33:01.622
It narrowed the gap
between the rich and poor.

00:33:01.622 --> 00:33:04.044
The immigrants and non-immigrants.

00:33:04.044 --> 00:33:08.294
So, we're caring about kids
lives, their wellbeing.

00:33:12.142 --> 00:33:16.212
And again, we want to
inspire them to think

00:33:16.212 --> 00:33:19.354
about the contributions they wanna make.

00:33:19.354 --> 00:33:23.232
I'm worried kids aren't gonna
wanna come back to school

00:33:23.232 --> 00:33:24.912
after the pandemic.

00:33:24.912 --> 00:33:26.153
They're gonna feel behind.

00:33:26.153 --> 00:33:29.903
Lets get them inspired
by a contribution that

00:33:30.808 --> 00:33:31.903
they hope to make.

00:33:31.903 --> 00:33:34.453
Let's get them ready to do it.

00:33:34.453 --> 00:33:36.303
Let's keep them in school.

00:33:36.303 --> 00:33:40.080
Let's make schools they want to be in.

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:43.891
In order to learn how to
make their contribution.

00:33:43.891 --> 00:33:47.236
And I think just one more thing.

00:33:47.236 --> 00:33:50.791
I think the pandemic has
taught so many teachers

00:33:50.791 --> 00:33:54.374
how much caring about
the students matters.

00:33:56.661 --> 00:34:00.101
Being part of their lives matters.

00:34:00.101 --> 00:34:03.414
How much being, integrating the school

00:34:03.414 --> 00:34:05.664
with the community matters.

00:34:06.501 --> 00:34:09.751
And, not just the teaching and testing.

00:34:10.944 --> 00:34:15.944
But creating a culture that
makes students love learning.

00:34:16.354 --> 00:34:18.021
And learn optimally.

00:34:21.104 --> 00:34:22.142
- [Sal] Thanks, that's so inspiring.

00:34:22.142 --> 00:34:22.975
Vicky.

00:34:24.042 --> 00:34:26.800
- I guess reinforcing what Carol says is.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:29.207
I want to say it can be done.

00:34:29.207 --> 00:34:32.267
Yes, it can be done in the most isolated

00:34:32.267 --> 00:34:36.580
and the most impoverished
areas of many of our countries.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:38.187
It can be done.

00:34:38.187 --> 00:34:41.313
We demonstrated that
with the UNESCO study.

00:34:41.313 --> 00:34:43.276
The first comparative international study

00:34:43.276 --> 00:34:45.245
of UNESCO in Latin America.

00:34:45.245 --> 00:34:47.834
Years back, I think it
was in the year 2000.

00:34:47.834 --> 00:34:50.577
Columbia and Cuba were
the only two countries

00:34:50.577 --> 00:34:53.629
where rural schools
outperformed urban schools.

00:34:53.629 --> 00:34:54.993
Except in the mega cities.

00:34:54.993 --> 00:34:58.225
Demonstrated that yes,
we can reduce inequity.

00:34:58.225 --> 00:34:59.357
It's possible.

00:34:59.357 --> 00:35:01.357
But also, not only that.

00:35:03.333 --> 00:35:07.755
But it compensates for
social economic inequalities.

00:35:07.755 --> 00:35:09.578
This is what we wanted to demonstrate.

00:35:09.578 --> 00:35:10.910
That it can be done.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.404
But also, the children learn.

00:35:13.404 --> 00:35:15.309
And the children love learning.

00:35:15.309 --> 00:35:16.225
And they continue.

00:35:16.225 --> 00:35:19.585
But because more of
the same is not enough.

00:35:19.585 --> 00:35:20.865
I think this is important.

00:35:20.865 --> 00:35:24.137
We really have to make
this change in education.

00:35:24.137 --> 00:35:27.064
An I'm quoting someone that I heard.

00:35:27.064 --> 00:35:28.494
And I always say, mention it.

00:35:28.494 --> 00:35:30.672
Because people do not understand.

00:35:30.672 --> 00:35:34.241
"If we bring a doctor
from a hundred years back

00:35:34.241 --> 00:35:38.221
into a hospital today,
that doctor is lost."

00:35:38.221 --> 00:35:41.886
Because everything has
changed permanently so much.

00:35:41.886 --> 00:35:44.275
But if we bring a teacher
from a hundred years ago

00:35:44.275 --> 00:35:46.961
into a classroom today.

00:35:46.961 --> 00:35:50.775
Even in an elite school,
that teacher is not so lost.

00:35:50.775 --> 00:35:53.773
Because everything has changed
except the way of learning.

00:35:53.773 --> 00:35:56.062
So this is why this is
the powerful message.

00:35:56.062 --> 00:35:57.431
It can be done.

00:35:57.431 --> 00:35:59.970
But more of the same, it's not enough.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:03.031
We have to make these changes.

00:36:03.031 --> 00:36:03.864
- No.

00:36:03.864 --> 00:36:05.662
Well, thank you both for
spending the time with us.

00:36:05.662 --> 00:36:08.276
Honestly, for your decades
of incredible work.

00:36:08.276 --> 00:36:09.973
Which I think is just getting started

00:36:09.973 --> 00:36:12.141
on the impact on humanity.

00:36:12.141 --> 00:36:16.235
I wanna special thanks to
"The Yidan Price Foundation."

00:36:16.235 --> 00:36:19.101
Which has a mission of
creating a better world

00:36:19.101 --> 00:36:20.667
through education.

00:36:20.667 --> 00:36:22.762
Supports practices and education

00:36:22.762 --> 00:36:25.102
for putting us all together.

00:36:25.102 --> 00:36:28.597
We're all on this, what they
call "council of luminaries."

00:36:28.597 --> 00:36:29.886
Which we're proud to serve on.

00:36:29.886 --> 00:36:32.326
But, I looked at both of your work

00:36:32.326 --> 00:36:33.906
for inspiration all the time.

00:36:33.906 --> 00:36:35.256
When we're thinking through Khan Academy.

00:36:35.256 --> 00:36:38.636
As you know, I was more
on listening mode today.

00:36:38.636 --> 00:36:39.795
But everything that we stand for

00:36:39.795 --> 00:36:41.638
is essentially what y'all stand for.

00:36:41.638 --> 00:36:43.116
How do we give students agency?

00:36:43.116 --> 00:36:45.756
How do we ensure that
teachers have the information

00:36:45.756 --> 00:36:49.242
so that they can do more
focused work with students.

00:36:49.242 --> 00:36:50.795
More human work with students.

00:36:50.795 --> 00:36:52.529
Students can learn at
their own time and pace.

00:36:52.529 --> 00:36:54.695
You need to measure, you need to assess.

00:36:54.695 --> 00:36:57.356
But it shouldn't be a
statement on who you are.

00:36:57.356 --> 00:36:59.806
It should just be a
statement on where you're at.

00:36:59.806 --> 00:37:01.619
And so this, even the
social of mastery learning.

00:37:01.619 --> 00:37:04.013
And if you're at an 80%, not a big deal.

00:37:04.013 --> 00:37:05.331
Keep working on it.

00:37:05.331 --> 00:37:06.403
Get to a 90%.

00:37:06.403 --> 00:37:07.964
Get to a 100%.

00:37:07.964 --> 00:37:10.055
Which it feels very closely aligned

00:37:10.055 --> 00:37:13.079
with this notion of everything y'all do.

00:37:13.079 --> 00:37:16.746
So thank you so much
for being part of this.

00:37:17.785 --> 00:37:19.097
- Wonderful to be with you, Sal.

00:37:19.097 --> 00:37:21.595
Wonderful to have met you a bit in person.

00:37:21.595 --> 00:37:22.428
Thank you.

00:37:22.428 --> 00:37:25.129
And hope to meet you someday in person.

00:37:25.129 --> 00:37:26.368
- Nice to see you again.

00:37:26.368 --> 00:37:30.721
- I'm going to give a
nice Latino "abrazo".

00:37:30.721 --> 00:37:32.651
- Great to see you again, Sal.

00:37:32.651 --> 00:37:34.651
- Great to see you also.

00:37:36.565 --> 00:37:38.564
- Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:37:38.564 --> 00:37:39.397
As you can tell.

00:37:39.397 --> 00:37:41.022
We could have spoken for several hours to

00:37:41.022 --> 00:37:43.045
each of Carol and Vicky.

00:37:43.045 --> 00:37:45.479
They really are titans in
the field of education.

00:37:45.479 --> 00:37:48.938
And it actually turns out
that a couple of years ago.

00:37:48.938 --> 00:37:50.138
I was at a restaurant.

00:37:50.138 --> 00:37:54.159
And I saw them having, I'm
pretty sure it was Carol.

00:37:54.159 --> 00:37:55.676
And it might've been Vicky as well.

00:37:55.676 --> 00:37:59.129
And I was just like, "Oh,
they won't wanna talk to me."

00:37:59.129 --> 00:37:59.962
But just right before this.

00:37:59.962 --> 00:38:01.351
I said, "Hey, I saw
you at the restaurant."

00:38:01.351 --> 00:38:02.497
Like, "Why didn't you come by?"

00:38:02.497 --> 00:38:04.456
I was like, "I didn't
want to bother y'all."

00:38:04.456 --> 00:38:05.289
But anyway.

00:38:05.289 --> 00:38:07.115
They're two heroes of mine.

00:38:07.115 --> 00:38:08.946
They've done profound work.

00:38:08.946 --> 00:38:10.447
That's already impacted the world.

00:38:10.447 --> 00:38:12.589
And I think is going to
only impact the world

00:38:12.589 --> 00:38:15.909
more and more over the decades
and generations to come.

00:38:15.909 --> 00:38:16.742
And as I mentioned.

00:38:16.742 --> 00:38:18.536
Everything we do here at Khan Academy

00:38:18.536 --> 00:38:21.983
is about trying to empower
exactly what they're up to.

00:38:21.983 --> 00:38:24.326
In order for someone to
have a "growth mindset"

00:38:24.326 --> 00:38:25.159
about learning.

00:38:25.159 --> 00:38:26.900
They should be able to learn
at their own time and pace.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:28.087
If they haven't mastered it.

00:38:28.087 --> 00:38:29.725
Keep working on it.

00:38:29.725 --> 00:38:32.026
That the role of the
classroom should not be

00:38:32.026 --> 00:38:35.109
one way flow of information, lecture.

00:38:36.119 --> 00:38:37.816
Everyone sitting passively silent.

00:38:37.816 --> 00:38:39.953
It should be everyone working together.

00:38:39.953 --> 00:38:41.228
Students working with each other.

00:38:41.228 --> 00:38:42.659
The teacher walking around.

00:38:42.659 --> 00:38:44.103
Forming human connections.

00:38:44.103 --> 00:38:46.482
We should create ability
for students to have agency

00:38:46.482 --> 00:38:48.533
over their own learning.

00:38:48.533 --> 00:38:50.550
Which might be actually the
most important skill of all.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:53.566
Even more important than
any of the academic skills.

00:38:53.566 --> 00:38:55.413
So thank you for joining.

00:38:55.413 --> 00:38:58.836
And hopefully you enjoyed
that conversation.

00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:01.419
(upbeat music)

